@@ -124,6 +124,25 @@ If you're using a provider other than AWS, you will also need some of the follow
 If your storage provider is on a different site than your Fizzy instance and doesn't return CORS headers on presigned URL responses, inline images may fail to load.
 In that case, set `SERVICE_WORKER_CORS_ENABLED=false` so the service worker fetches uploaded files without CORS mode.
 
+#### Web push notifications
+
+To enable push notifications, provide a matching `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and
+`VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` as environment variables. Generate a pair once with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'
+```
+
+Keep the private key secret and retain the same pair across deployments: browser
+subscriptions are tied to the public key. For Kamal deployments, add both keys
+to `env.secret` and your secrets provider. Push is unavailable when either key
+is missing; email and tray notifications continue to work.
+
+Serve the app over HTTPS (localhost also works for development), and run the
+background job worker. Each person can enable push for the browser/device they
+are using in **Notification Settings**. On iOS, install Fizzy as a web app first.
+Push notifications use the tray's text and link to the relevant card or comment.
+
 #### Multi-tenant mode
 
 By default, when you run the Fizzy Docker image you'll be limited to creating a single account (although that account can have as many users as you like).
